Upon further research, what I've got here is the Master Routine for
performing subroutine calls.
The 'cal Y' instruction is equivalent to jda 100: jump deposit
accumulator.
It copies the AC to mem[100], then jumps to 101. So the label here is
hardcoded into the processor!

  buf,
  77/      0
              0          /This is location 100, which holds AC
(argument)
              dap rx /This is location 101, implicitly jmped to by the
'cal' instuction
                           /we deposit the return address in rx  (the
exit line of car)
              sub (1  /subtracting one gives the address of the 'cal'
instruction itself
              dap .+1  /save this address into the load instruction
just below
              lac xy   /follow the pointer
              dap ave+1  /exit to this address when we exit
              lac rx   /reload the return address into acc.
              jda pwl /jmp to 'push word on push-down list'
  ave,     lac 100  /load argument back into AC
              exit       /jmp to the subroutine

