There is a chip on the underside of the main logic board that has a
rep for causing 'heat death'.  If you do searches on "Multia heat
death" I'm sure there are still pages online about the issue, and
means to correct it (chip replacement).

In lieu of that I installed a fan in the Multia base blowing up and
clipped the sensor for the Multia case fan (so it ran at full speed
always).  Can't say if it made a difference but neither of my units
ever had heat death problems, even when the computer room at home got
decidedly hot with a VAXstation, AS600, monitors, comm gear, VT
terminal, and Multia running in it (90 degrees was the best the A/C
could do with that on bad days).  Multi-week uptimes with daytime room
temps getting into the high 80s was normal.


